  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nThis strategy implements a synergistic hybrid of Bayesian optimization and constraint-handling techniques with a memorized penalty structure. The core principle is to leverage the step-function space structure while dynamically adapting penalties and fitness evaluations. We search across multiple template families: uniform, Gaussian, ramp (increasing/decreasing), and bimodal representations, combining them with deterministic pattern mutations and simplex projection to avoid local minima.\n\nThe key difference from the original mirrordescent approach is the use of deterministic convex combinations that redistribute values predictably. We address the exotic LP solutions by imposing smooth boundaries with a Frank-Wolfe convexification step. The attacker also explicitly monitors the scoring envelope to balance between aggressive mutations and preservation of promising features.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n- Normalization: Sequences set initially to have sum approaching (2/3)*sqrt(n), based on observed peak efficiencies.\n- Objective: E[seq] = 2n * max(conv(seq)) / (sum(seq))^2.\n- Constraints: Each value \u2208 [0, 1000].\n- Representation: Discretized by length and template, but optimized as continuous multivariate functions.\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Generate diverse sequences using the specified templates, with length drawn from [64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048].\n   - Combine with the parent sequence interpolation (if available) to maintain consistency.\n\n2. Fitness Evaluation:\n   - Compute E(\u00b7) for each sequence, handling divisi\u00f3n by small sums and NaN values properly.\n   - Double-check normalization: unnormalize by factoring out (median sum factor).\n\n3. Updating the Best Candidate:\n   - After each evaluation, check for improvement in either the objective or diversity.\n   - Allow improvement even if only slightly. Avoid greedy optimization.\n\n4. Template-Based Mutation:\n   - Create new sequences by crossing templates at strategic points.\n   - Use magnitude-based selection: prioritize patterns that have high and low peaks.\n\n5. Convexification (Mirror Descent):\n   - For the best candidate, perform a convex combination with a more uniform background.\n   - This avoids excessive hill climbing by maintaining smoothness.\n\n6. Progressive Deepening:\n   - Test increasing template innovations by step-wise variation.\n   - Monitor time and interrupt aggressively when time is running low.\n\n7. Exhaustive Mutation:\n   - When time allows, explore a wider mutation landscape.\n   - Use an iterative process that tries multiple variants simultaneously.\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n- The primary templates are uniform, Gaussian, and bimodal along different axes.\n- Length choices are restricted to powers of two for FFT compatibility.\n- Optimization uses Simpson curves for the search space boundaries.\n\nComputebudget and Robustness:\n- We perform 20 main initial checks per time phase.\n- FFT computation limited to lengths under 2048.\n- We define a max sum of 0.01 to conserve coefficients.\n\nValidation and Return Contract:\n- The returned sequence must be non-negative, with all values within [0, 1000].\n- We evaluate and update the best candidate at every stage, ensuring validity.\n- In case of failures, we return a fallback sequence.\n\nLet's put it all together step-by-step in the code.",
